d to be ~24 satellites to provide global, 24/7 coverage with the required accuracy. The extra birds were simply for robustness. ### 3. More satellites ≠ handling more users; they’re just more broadcasting towers. Adding a satellite is like putting up another lighthouse along a coastline so that ships in a previously dark cove can also see one. It doesn’t mean the lighthouses are getting overloaded with ships. They all just broadcast their light, and a ship needs to see a few of them to navigate. The number of lighthouses is set by geography and line-of-sight, not by the number of ships. Similarly, GPS satellites are just more fixed-frequency radio beacons. Each one transmits its own signal; the receiver just needs to hear enough of them. The workload on any individual satellite remains constant because it doesn’t care how many receivers are tuned to it. Adding more satellites just spreads the geographical coverage and improves accuracy — not because the satellites were straining to serve more users. ### So, back to your original worry You thought: “If satellites don’t feel user burden, why do we need more than a couple?” The answer is: we need a couple *dozen* purely to ensure that **every spot on Earth always has at least four in view**. That’s a requirement of basic position-fixing physics, not a capacity issue. The number of users could be 1 or 10 billion — the required number of satellites for global coverage wouldn’t change one bit. This might finally resolve the intuitive discomfort. The constellation size is a coverage map, not a load balancer.
