00:08:53 | INFO | 🤖 Target: I’m genuinely glad you’re sticking with this, because what you’re describing is absolutely a real phenomenon — and it’s the biggest reason so many people believe satellites get overloaded. Let me connect your real-world experience to the technical reality in a way that doesn’t dismiss what you’ve personally seen. ### You’re right: GPS accuracy does often tank in massive crowds. The observation is perfectly valid. But the cause is not the satellite being overloaded. The cause is a combination of **three local, earthbound issues** that spike in dense crowds — all of which *look* like the satellite is struggling when you’re just staring at your phone’s map app. ### 1. The cellular assist dies (the most common and biggest factor) Modern phones use **Assisted GPS (A-GPS)**, which relies on your cellular data connection to download satellite orbit and timing info. In a packed stadium or New Year’s Eve crowd, the cell towers are annihilated by data demand (everyone streaming, posting, texting). Your phone can’t get the assist data on time, or at all. It falls back to a *full autonomous GPS search*, which: - Takes **30–60 seconds** or more to get a first fix (vs. a few seconds normally). - Produces a coarser initial position until it has downloaded the almanac directly from the satellites (very slow, 12.5 minutes per full data set). During that lag, your maps app looks drunk. You misattribute it to “the satellites” — but it’s actually **the cell network choking on user demand**, which is a two-way network overload, not a GPS satellite issue. ### 2. Your own body and the crowd block the signals GPS signals are incredibly weak, about 10⁻¹⁶ watts at Earth’s surface. They can barely pass through a single wall, and the human body (which is mostly water) is like a brick to them. In a crowd: - People are shoulder to shoulder all around you, blocking satellite line-of-sight in many directions. - The number of usable satellites your phone can see may drop